<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Scriba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1154">1154</a>–<a href="/years/1164">1164</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Scriba was a Genoese notary of the twelfth century whose surviving register, covering the years 1154 to 1164, is the oldest notarial cartulary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its more than a thousand contracts record the commenda partnerships, loans, sales and marriage agreements of Genoese merchants trading with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, and it is a key source for the medieval commercial revolution.</p></div>
